338
00:46:28.220 --> 00:46:45.799
Drummond Reed: Now, once you do that, because you've done it all, and you can do the whole thing, you know, essentially locally, in a wallet, you now have the initial DID document, and then if you want to rotate keys, or change endpoints, or do anything to produce a subsequent version of the DID document.

339
00:46:46.350 --> 00:47:00.580
Drummond Reed: all of them fall the same thing, which is you basically start to chain them together, and the next DID document contains a hash of the first one, and is signed by a key in the first one, and now you've got your second state.

340
00:47:00.770 --> 00:47:07.630
Drummond Reed: And the combination of those did documents, is now widely being called your verifiable history.

341
00:47:08.080 --> 00:47:09.309
Drummond Reed: Does that make sense?

